WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.624
What each provider documents (vendor claims).

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:06.339
These are the providers’ own descriptions at the time of review.

3
00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:08.859
They are claims to test, not findings.

4
00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:10.461
Anthropic (Claude).

5
00:00:10.741 --> 00:00:13.557
The models overview recommends Claude Opus 5.

6
00:00:13.837 --> 00:00:20.856
5 as the starting point for most workloads (“long-running agentic coding and knowledge work”), Claude Fable 5.

7
00:00:21.136 --> 00:00:26.043
2 for demanding reasoning and long-horizon agentic work, Claude Sonnet 5.

8
00:00:26.323 --> 00:00:30.355
5 as the speed and intelligence balance, and Claude Haiku 5.

9
00:00:30.635 --> 00:00:36.864
5 for high-volume, latency-sensitive tasks such as classification, extraction, and routing.

10
00:00:37.144 --> 00:00:45.805
All four list 1M-token context windows and 128K output tokens, accept text and images, and produce text.

11
00:00:46.085 --> 00:00:47.664
Prices range from 0.

12
00:00:47.944 --> 00:00:52.765
10 dollars (input) per million tokens on Haiku to 8 dollars on Fable.

13
00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:59.488
The documentation leans on agentic coding, tool use, and controls such as effort and prompt caching.

14
00:00:59.768 --> 00:01:01.624
OpenAI (GPT).

15
00:01:01.904 --> 00:01:09.520
The models page features GPT-6 Astra as the flagship for complex reasoning and coding, GPT-6.

16
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.237
1 Sol as a lower-cost model for complex work, GPT-6.

17
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:23.563
1 Terra for visual reasoning, precise computer use, and natural writing, and GPT-6 Luna for cost-sensitive, high-volume workloads.

18
00:01:23.843 --> 00:01:25.997
All list context windows of about 1.

19
00:01:26.277 --> 00:01:33.872
05M tokens, accept text and images, and support function calling, web search, file search, and computer use.

20
00:01:34.152 --> 00:01:35.731
Prices range from 0.

21
00:01:36.011 --> 00:01:40.853
10 dollars (Luna input) to 10 dollars (Astra input) per million tokens.

22
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.413
Google (Gemini).

23
00:01:42.693 --> 00:02:02.619
The models page describes Gemini 4 Carbon as its strongest reasoning model (complex software engineering, scientific reasoning, cybersecurity) and Gemini 4 Argon as efficient frontier reasoning, both with 1M-token windows and native input of text, images, audio, video, and PDF.

24
00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:13.117
Flash and Flash-Lite models cover speed and throughput, and the catalog includes dedicated live-audio, speech, image, video, embedding, and deep-research models.

25
00:02:13.397 --> 00:02:18.453
Native multimodality across audio and video is the documented differentiator.

26
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:20.077
xAI (Grok).

27
00:02:20.357 --> 00:02:22.576
The models page recommends Grok 4.

28
00:02:22.856 --> 00:02:34.739
7 for code and chat, with a 500K-token window (some other Grok models list 1M), and long-context pricing that doubles once a prompt reaches 200K tokens.

29
00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:50.357
The distinctive feature is real-time data: the docs state Grok has no knowledge of events after training unless you enable the Web Search or X Search tools, and X Search queries posts, users, and threads on X and returns citations.
